<BaseLayout title="Early-Universe Gravitational Waves — Jonas El Gammal" description="SIGWAY, LISA, and reconstruction of primordial curvature perturbations.">
  <div class="shell"><header class="page-head"><span class="eyebrow">Research · Early universe</span><h1>Listening for primordial structure.</h1><p>Scalar-induced gravitational waves may let LISA probe curvature perturbations and inflationary dynamics on otherwise inaccessible scales.</p></header></div>
  <article class="prose"><h2>Reconstructing the curvature spectrum</h2><p>As part of the LISA Cosmology Working Group, I contributed to <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.11320">Reconstructing Primordial Curvature Perturbations via Scalar-Induced Gravitational Waves with LISA</a>. We compare agnostic binned reconstruction, physically motivated templates, and first-principles inference of inflationary models.</p><figure class="figure"><img src="/assets/img/detector_sensitivity.svg" alt="Sensitivity of gravitational-wave detectors to primordial curvature perturbations"/><figcaption>Detector sensitivity to enhanced primordial curvature perturbations.</figcaption></figure><h2>SIGWAY</h2><p><a href="https://github.com/jonaselgammal/SIGWAY"><strong>SIGWAY</strong></a> is the open-source analysis framework behind this work. It composes a primordial spectrum, a cosmological transfer kernel, and a numerical integrator into an induced gravitational-wave spectrum.</p><p>For binned spectra, SIGWAY ships precomputed bilinear coefficients. This makes the mapping fast enough for inference—and for the interactive laboratory below.</p></article>
